-- title:
--   Decomposition order equals the number of rational automorphisms of (E,C)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime subject to $q = 3$, $M'$ is a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell_g$ is a prime with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$.
--
--   *Base valuation and the supersingular place.* $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ in the sense that $q$ is a non-unit of $A$ (`hA`); write $\kappa$ for its residue field. The finite set $W$ is required by `hW` to consist exactly of the supersingular places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$ (the subfield of $\kappa((q))$ generated over $\kappa$ by the $j$-expansion and its $M'$-dilate), i.e. of those places $w$ which are rational (the structure map $\kappa \to w.\mathrm{ResidueField}$ is surjective), satisfy the predicate `IsAffineGeomPlace`, and whose value $w.\mathrm{evalAt}$ at `jGeomGen` lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,q\,\kappa$, the set of $j \in \kappa$ such that every elliptic curve over $\kappa$ with invariant $j$ has no nonzero point killed by $q$. An element $s \in W$ is fixed. The hypothesis `hle` asks that the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the level-$M'$ full modular function field be contained in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M' = \mathrm{xHFunctionFieldBar}(q^2M',\ \mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M')$, where $\mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'$ is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$. Further, $R_0$ is a constant reduction of $A$ from $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$ (a valuation subring `integers`, a surjective residue homomorphism with kernel the maximal ideal, a map on places preserving degrees, and the compatibility clauses of the structure `ConstantReduction`), and `hR₀` requires that every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and that its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $\kappa$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   *Constant field.* $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ an element of $A$; put $A_0 := A \cap k_0$ (the comap of $A$ along $k_0 \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$). The hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` state that $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, henselian, and with algebraically closed residue field, and `hκ` states that every $a \in A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to some element of $k_0 \cap A$.
--
--   *Auxiliary data.* A prime $\ell$ with $\ell \ge 3$, $\ell \ne q$, $\ell \nmid M'$ is given, together with $\zeta_0 \in k_0$ a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and $\varpi_t \in k_0 \cap A$ with $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$ (`hϖt`). Next, $K_b$ is an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/k_0$ required to be $\bot$, $A_b$ a valuation subring of $K_b$ cut out by $A$ (`hAb`), and $\varpi_b \ne 0$ a generator of the maximal ideal of $A_b$. The tameness hypothesis `htame` asks that $\ell_g - 1$ be a unit of $A_0$, and $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity.
--
--   *Level and chart.* $H_1$ is the subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ equal to $\mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'$ intersected with the kernel of reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$, and $K_\ell$ is the $k_0$-base change of the $\mathbb Q$-rational function field $\mathrm{xHFunctionField}(q^2M', H_1)$ inside $k_0((q))$; $j_\ell \in K_\ell$ is the element whose Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of `jq`, and is nonzero. An element $\varpi_t' \in A_0$ with image $\varpi_t$ in $k_0$ is fixed. Write $C := \mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A_0\,K_\ell\,j_\ell$ for the subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $A_0[j_\ell]$.
--
--   *The closed point $y$.* $y$ is a maximal ideal of $C$ containing the image of $\pi_0$ (`hϖy`). The hypothesis `hss` requires that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j_\ell)$ lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,q\,\Omega$. The hypothesis `hover` expresses that $y$ lies over $s$: for every $g$ in the full level-$M'$ modular function field over $\mathbb Q$ whose base change lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$, which is regular at every place $P$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ at which the base change of `jq` is regular ($0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}$ of the latter implies $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}(g)$), and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and for every $c \in k_0 \cap A$ whose residue in $\kappa$ equals $s.\mathrm{evalAt}$ of that $R_0$-residue, the difference $g - c$, taken in $C$, lies in $y$. The hypothesis `hOrbit` requires that any maximal ideal $y'$ of $C$ containing $\pi_0$ and satisfying the same condition (the clause of `hover` with $y$ replaced by $y'$) be of the form obtained from $y$ by a level automorphism: there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and a $k_0$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K_\ell$ with $\mathrm{IsLevelAutAt}\,k_0\,q\,\xi\,q\,(q^2M')\,H_1\,\gamma^{-1}\,K_\ell\,\tau$ — that is, for every embedding $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/q}$ and every presentation of an element of $K_\ell$ as a quotient of two weight-$k$ modular forms for $\mathrm{GammaH}(q^2M', H_1)$ with integral $q$-expansions, $\tau$ acts on that element by the weight-$k$ slash action of the matrix $\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$ — such that $\tau$ preserves $C$ and, for $f \in C$, $f \in y \iff \tau f \in y'$.
--
--   *The rigid supersingular chart.* $B_t$ is an $A_0$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$, $W_t$ a valuation subring of $K_\ell$ with $B_t \subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`). The hypothesis `hS1` consists of fourteen clauses: $C \le B_t$; $K_\ell$ is the fraction field of $B_t$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A_0$; $B_t/\pi_0 B_t$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; $W_t \cap k_0$ is the image of $A_0$; the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is generated by $\pi_0$; $W_t$ is a discrete valuation ring; $y$ is the contraction to $C$ of the maximal ideal of $W_t$; $W_t$ is the localisation of $B_t$ at that maximal ideal; a Drinfeld-curve clause requiring, for every $\mathbb F_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $\kappa_0$ of $A_0$, a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho$ from $B_t$ onto $\mathrm{DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing}\,q\,\kappa_0$ whose kernel is the set of elements of $B_t$ in the maximal ideal of $W_t$, which is $A_0$-equivariant for the residue map, and which is equivariant for level automorphisms in the following sense: for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ there is $c \in (\mathbb F_{q^2})^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c)$ in $\mathrm{hSubgroup}\,q$ (the kernel of $(\gamma,c) \mapsto \det\gamma \cdot c^{q+1}$) such that $\rho \circ \tau = \mathrm{hAction}$ of that pair applied to $\rho$, with $c \ne 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity, and with the scalar $c$ realised again by some $\gamma' \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphism $\tau'$ preserving $W_t$ through the pair $(1, c)$; stability of $B_t$ under all level automorphisms for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$; the requirement that every prime ideal $Q$ of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ receive the centre of $W_t$ under some level automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$; and the requirement that $b \in B_t$ be divisible by $\pi_0$ as soon as all its images under such level automorphisms lie in the maximal ideal of $W_t$.
--
--   *The cyclic decomposition datum.* $n \ge 1$ is a natural number dividing $q+1$; $\gamma_0 \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau_0$ is a level automorphism at $\gamma_0^{-1}$ with $f \in W_t \iff \tau_0 f \in W_t$. Further, $m \ge 1$ satisfies $\pi_0^m = \varpi_t' w$ for a unit $w$ of $A_0$ (`hm`), and $\zeta_c$ in the adic completion of $A_0$ satisfies $\zeta_c^n = 1$ with $\zeta_c^k - 1$ a unit for $0 < k < n$. A finite set `ends` of subrings of $K_\ell$ is given, and the hypothesis `hR7` consists of ten clauses: $\tau_0^n$ fixes $B_t$ pointwise; for $0 < k < n$ the automorphism $\tau_0^k$ moves some element of $B_t$; every level automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with some $\tau_0^k$, $k < n$; `ends` has exactly $q+1$ elements; level automorphisms with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ permute `ends`; this action is transitive on `ends`; each $O \in$ `ends` is preserved by $\tau_0$; distinct members of `ends` are separated by an element which is a non-unit in one and a unit in the other; a ninth clause attaching to each $O \in$ `ends` (summarised here) the facts that $O \subseteq W_t$, that $O$ is local and noetherian, contains $C$, meets $k_0$ in $A_0$, and has residue field generated by constants, together with an explicit crossing model — $\pi_0 \in O$, elements $c_x, c_y \in O$ and a unit $u$ with $c_xc_y = \pi_0^m u$, an isomorphism $\iota$ of the adic completion of $O$ with $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}$ over the adic completion of $A_0$ at parameter $\pi_0^m$ (the quotient of the two-variable power series ring by $UV - \pi_0^m$) sending constants to constants and $c_x, c_y$ to unit multiples of $U, V$, with $c_y$ in and $c_x$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_t$, an automorphism $\theta_0$ of the crossing model over the completed base conjugating $\tau_0$ through $\iota$ and satisfying $\theta_0(U) \equiv \zeta_c U$, $\theta_0(V) \equiv \zeta_c' V$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal (where $\zeta_c\zeta_c' = 1$) — and the existence of a second branch $W_x$ (a discrete valuation subring of $K_\ell$ containing $O$ and $C$, meeting $k_0$ in $A_0$, with uniformiser $\pi_0$, carrying a parameter $t$ at which no polynomial over $A_0$ with some unit coefficient vanishes modulo the maximal ideal, with $c_x$ in and $c_y$ outside its maximal ideal, and cutting out on $C$ an ideal contained in but different from $y$) and of a finite-type $A_0$-subalgebra $B_x \subseteq B_t \cap O$, stable under all level automorphisms with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, consisting of elements regular wherever $j_\ell$ is, and with $O$ the localisation of $B_x$ at its non-units; and a tenth clause stating that any valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ meeting $k_0$ in $A_0$, containing $C$ and centred on $y$ either contains $B_t$ or dominates some $O \in$ `ends`. The hypothesis `hR5d` states that a level automorphism for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $y$ on $C$ preserves $W_t$.
--
--   *The moduli interpretation of $s$.* Finally, $E$ is an elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\kappa$ and $\mathrm{Cyc}$ an additive subgroup of its group of affine points which is cyclic (`hcyc`) of order $M'$ (`hcardCyc`), and `hP` identifies the valuation subring of $s$ with the pullback, along the inclusion of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$ into the full field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFullC}\,\kappa\,M'$, of the valuation subring of the moduli place $\mathrm{moduliPlace}\,\kappa\,M'\,E\,\mathrm{Cyc}$.
--
--   *Conclusion.* Under these hypotheses, $n$ equals the cardinality (`Nat.card`) of the set of additive endomorphisms $\iota$ of the group of affine points of $E$ such that: $\iota$ lies in $\mathrm{WeierstrassCurve.rationalHomSet}\,\kappa\,E\,E$, i.e. $\iota = 0$ or $\iota$ is rationally represented (there are bivariate polynomials $n_X, d_X, n_Y, d_Y$ over $\kappa$ and a finite set $B$ of excluded $x$-coordinates outside of which $\iota$ sends a nonsingular point $(x,y)$ to $(n_X/d_X, n_Y/d_Y)$ evaluated at $(x,y)$, with nonvanishing denominators); there is $\iota'$ in the same set with $\iota' \circ \iota$ and $\iota \circ \iota'$ both the identity; and $\iota$ carries $\mathrm{Cyc}$ onto $\mathrm{Cyc}$, i.e. $\mathrm{Cyc}.\mathrm{map}\,\iota = \mathrm{Cyc}$. In other words, the order $n$ of the cyclic group of level automorphisms fixing the supersingular chart equals the number of rational automorphisms of the pair $(E, \mathrm{Cyc})$ over $\kappa$.
--
--   This is the order count identifying the cyclic decomposition group attached to the supersingular chart above the place $s$ with the automorphism group of the corresponding pair $(E, C_{M'})$ over the residue field $\kappa$, in the $q = 3$, $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-guarded frame with the scalar clause linked into the Drinfeld-coordinate block. It is cited in the companion step which re-expresses the same order $n$ as twice the place-width quantity, on the way to the ramification count for the semistable model at $q$.
